# Alerting credentials for hft-collector units.
#
# Copy to /opt/hft-collector/etc/alert.env, own it root:root, chmod 600:
#
#   sudo install -o root -g root -m 600 alert.env.example /opt/hft-collector/etc/alert.env
#   sudo $EDITOR /opt/hft-collector/etc/alert.env
#
# This file is OPERATOR-AUTHORED and never lives in git with real values.
# Without it (or with placeholders), alert.sh logs "alert not sent" and exits
# cleanly — the OnFailure hook is safe to fire on an unconfigured host, so the
# main unit template wires it unconditionally.

# --- Telegram push alerts (unit failures, red gate days) -------------------
# Create a bot via @BotFather, add it to your alerts group, then read the
# group's chat id from the bot's update queue:
#   curl -s "https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates"
# A group id is NEGATIVE. If the group is later upgraded to a supergroup,
# Telegram CHANGES its id (to -100...) and alerts silently stop — re-read the
# id after any such upgrade.
TG_BOT_TOKEN=123456789:REPLACE-ME
TG_CHAT_ID=-100REPLACE-ME

# --- Dead-man heartbeat (absence of good news) -----------------------------
# A healthchecks.io check URL (period 1 day, grace ~2h suggested). The daily
# quality gate pings it on success and hits <url>/fail on a red day; if the
# whole host dies silently, the missed ping raises the alarm — the one class
# of failure OnFailure= can never see. Leave empty to disable.
HEALTHCHECK_PING_URL=
